---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Experiment 3.1: Constructing 3-D Atomic Models">
    <main class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8">
        <div class="experiment-header">
            <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-center text-blue-700 mb-2">EXPERIMENT 3.1</h1>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-center text-blue-700 mb-8">CONSTRUCTING 3-D ATOMIC MODELS</h2>
        </div>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">PURPOSE:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">To create models of isotopes of an element.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">BACKGROUND</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Scientists and engineers use models all the time. Remember from module 1 that a model is a 
                useful simplification used to make it easier to understand things that might be too difficult to 
                directly observe. Good models also allow scientists to make and test predictions. As always in 
                science, models are changed when they no longer explain the data. The globe is a model of 
                the planet Earth. It wasn't until 2005 that the globe was made using actual satellite pictures 
                from NASA. There are computer models, such as weather models, in which scientists use 
                mathematical calculations to predict weather patterns. Engineers build models of bridges or 
                buildings to determine what materials will best suit the parameters of the project. In this 
                investigation, you will build a model of 2 atoms and use them to compare their structure.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">MATERIALS:</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-4">
                <li>2 small Styrofoam balls (Balls should be about 2 inches in diameter. Styrofoam balls from craft stores work well)</li>
                <li>Pipe cleaners (white or gray)</li>
                <li>Plastic pony beads (These can be found at craft stores)</li>
                <li>2 bamboo skewers</li>
                <li>Fishing line</li>
                <li>2 wire hangers</li>
                <li>Red and blue pushpins</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">QUESTION:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">How can the materials listed be used to show the difference between isotopes of an element?</p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">PROCEDURE:</h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-8 mb-4">
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Choose an element and analyze its isotopes:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Select one element from the table below</li>
                        <li>Use the table information to determine the number of protons, neutrons, and electrons</li>
                        <li>Choose 2 isotopes of your selected element (you can build all 3 if available)</li>
                        <li>Record this information and isotope names in your data table</li>
                    </ul>
                    
                    <div class="my-4 overflow-x-auto">
                        <table class="w-full bg-purple-100 border border-purple-300" style="table-layout: fixed;">
                            <thead>
                                <tr class="bg-purple-200">
                                    <th class="py-4 px-6 border-b border-purple-300 font-semibold" style="text-align: center; width: 25%;">Element</th>
                                    <th class="py-4 px-4 border-b border-purple-300 font-semibold border-r-2 border-purple-400" style="text-align: center; width: 20%;">Atomic Number</th>
                                    <th class="py-4 px-6 border-b border-purple-300 font-semibold" style="text-align: center; width: 55%;">Isotopes</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr class="hover:bg-purple-50">
                                    <td class="py-3 px-6 border-b border-purple-300 font-medium" style="text-align: center;">Nitrogen</td>
                                    <td class="py-3 px-4 border-b border-purple-300 border-r-2 border-purple-400" style="text-align: center;">7</td>
                                    <td class="py-3 border-b border-purple-300" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 3rem;">nitrogen-14, nitrogen-15</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr class="hover:bg-purple-50">
                                    <td class="py-3 px-6 border-b border-purple-300 font-medium" style="text-align: center;">Oxygen</td>
                                    <td class="py-3 px-4 border-b border-purple-300 border-r-2 border-purple-400" style="text-align: center;">8</td>
                                    <td class="py-3 border-b border-purple-300" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 3rem;">oxygen-16, oxygen-17, oxygen-18</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr class="hover:bg-purple-50">
                                    <td class="py-3 px-6 border-b border-purple-300 font-medium" style="text-align: center;">Magnesium</td>
                                    <td class="py-3 px-4 border-b border-purple-300 border-r-2 border-purple-400" style="text-align: center;">12</td>
                                    <td class="py-3 border-b border-purple-300" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 3rem;">magnesium-24, magnesium-25, magnesium-26</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr class="hover:bg-purple-50">
                                    <td class="py-3 px-6 border-b border-purple-300 font-medium" style="text-align: center;">Chlorine</td>
                                    <td class="py-3 px-4 border-b border-purple-300 border-r-2 border-purple-400" style="text-align: center;">17</td>
                                    <td class="py-3 border-b border-purple-300" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 3rem;">chlorine-35, chlorine-37</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Prepare your materials:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li><span class="text-red-600 font-semibold">Red pushpins</span> = protons</li>
                        <li><span class="text-blue-600 font-semibold">Blue pushpins</span> = neutrons</li>
                        <li><span class="text-gray-600 font-semibold">Plastic beads</span> = electrons</li>
                        <li>Set aside the correct number of each item for your isotopes</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Prepare the nucleus (Styrofoam ball):</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>The Styrofoam ball represents the nucleus area</li>
                        <li>Take a bamboo skewer and carefully insert it through the ball</li>
                        <li>Leave about 1 inch of skewer exposed on each side</li>
                        <li>Break or cut off any pieces longer than 1 inch</li>
                        <li class="text-orange-600 font-semibold">⚠️ Ask for help if needed - be careful with sharp objects!</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Build your isotope nuclei:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Insert the correct number of red and blue pushpins into one Styrofoam ball (first isotope)</li>
                        <li>Repeat with the second Styrofoam ball for your other isotope</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Attach nuclei to hangers:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Use fishing line to attach each nucleus to a wire hanger</li>
                        <li>Tie the fishing line around each end of the bamboo skewers</li>
                        <li>Then tie onto the hanger</li>
                        <li>Repeat for the second isotope</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Calculate energy levels:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Determine how many energy levels your isotopes have</li>
                        <li>Record this number in your data table</li>
                        <li class="bg-blue-50 p-2 rounded border-l-4 border-blue-400">
                            <strong>Energy Level Capacity:</strong>
                            <ul class="list-none ml-4 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                <li>• 1st level: holds <strong>2 electrons</strong></li>
                                <li>• 2nd level: holds <strong>8 electrons</strong></li>
                                <li>• 3rd level: holds <strong>18 electrons</strong></li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Construct energy levels with pipe cleaners:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Build the correct number of energy levels for one isotope</li>
                        <li class="bg-green-50 p-2 rounded border-l-4 border-green-400">
                            <strong>Pipe Cleaner Guide:</strong>
                            <ul class="list-none ml-4 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                <li>• 1st energy level: <strong>2-3 pipe cleaners</strong></li>
                                <li>• 2nd energy level: <strong>4-5 pipe cleaners</strong></li>
                                <li>• 3rd energy level: <strong>5-7 pipe cleaners</strong></li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>Twist the ends together to make each energy level</li>
                        <li><strong>Important:</strong> Don't close the circles yet - you need to add beads first!</li>
                        <li>Your model should look similar to the reference photo below:</li>
                    </ul>
                    
                    <div class="my-4 text-center">
                        <img src="/images/experiment3_1_setup.png" alt="Example of completed 3D atomic model showing nucleus with protons and neutrons, and electron energy levels" class="mx-auto max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg shadow-lg border border-gray-300">
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2 italic">Example of completed atomic model setup</p>
                    </div>
                    
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Add electrons to energy levels:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Determine the number of electrons in each energy level</li>
                        <li>Record this information in your data table</li>
                        <li>Place the appropriate number of beads on each pipe-cleaner circle</li>
                        <li>Close each circle by twisting the pipe-cleaners together</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Attach energy levels to hanger:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Use fishing line to attach energy levels to the hanger</li>
                        <li>Hang energy levels in the correct order</li>
                        <li>Position at the same level as the nucleus</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Complete second isotope:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Repeat steps 7-9 for your second isotope</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-4">
                    <strong>Clean up:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1">
                        <li>Put away all materials properly</li>
                        <li>Ensure your workspace is clean</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">CONCLUSIONS</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">
                In a conclusion paragraph, answer the following questions: How were the two models 
                you constructed similar? How were they different? Why did both of your models contain 
                the same number of protons and electrons? What are some ways your models were not 
                accurate in depicting the nucleus and electrons of an atom?
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">SUBMIT YOUR WORK</h3>
            <form id="experimentForm" class="bg-gray-100 p-6 rounded-lg">
                <div class="mb-4">
                    <label for="studentName" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Student Name:</label>
                    <input type="text" id="studentName" name="studentName" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg">
                </div>
                
                <div class="mb-4">
                    <label for="studentEmail" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Student Email:</label>
                    <input type="email" id="studentEmail" name="studentEmail" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg">
                </div>
                
                <div class="mb-4">
                    <label for="elementChosen" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Element Chosen:</label>
                    <select id="elementChosen" name="elementChosen" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg">
                        <option value="">Select an element</option>
                        <option value="Nitrogen">Nitrogen</option>
                        <option value="Oxygen">Oxygen</option>
                        <option value="Magnesium">Magnesium</option>
                        <option value="Chlorine">Chlorine</option>
                    </select>
                </div>
                
                <div class="mb-4">
                    <label for="isotopeAnalysis" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Isotope Analysis:</label>
                    <textarea id="isotopeAnalysis" name="isotopeAnalysis" rows="6" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg" placeholder="Example for Carbon:

Element: Carbon (Atomic Number 6)
Isotopes chosen: carbon-12 and carbon-14

Carbon-12:
- Protons: 6
- Neutrons: 6 (12-6)
- Electrons: 6
- Energy levels: 2 (first level: 2 electrons, second level: 4 electrons)

Carbon-14:
- Protons: 6
- Neutrons: 8 (14-6)
- Electrons: 6
- Energy levels: 2 (first level: 2 electrons, second level: 4 electrons)"></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="mb-4">
                    <label class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Data Table:</label>
                    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
                        <table class="w-full border border-gray-300 bg-white">
                            <thead>
                                <tr class="bg-gray-200">
                                    <th class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300 text-center font-semibold">Isotope</th>
                                    <th class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300 text-center font-semibold">Protons</th>
                                    <th class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300 text-center font-semibold">Neutrons</th>
                                    <th class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300 text-center font-semibold">Electrons</th>
                                    <th class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300 text-center font-semibold">Energy Levels</th>
                                    <th class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300 text-center font-semibold">Electrons/Level</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="text" id="isotope1" name="isotope1" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent" placeholder="e.g., nitrogen-14">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="number" id="protons1" name="protons1" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent text-center" placeholder="7">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="number" id="neutrons1" name="neutrons1" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent text-center" placeholder="7">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="number" id="electrons1" name="electrons1" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent text-center" placeholder="7">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="number" id="energyLevels1" name="energyLevels1" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent text-center" placeholder="2">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="text" id="electronsPerLevel1" name="electronsPerLevel1" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent" placeholder="2, 5">
                                    </td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="text" id="isotope2" name="isotope2" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent" placeholder="e.g., nitrogen-15">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="number" id="protons2" name="protons2" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent text-center" placeholder="7">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="number" id="neutrons2" name="neutrons2" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent text-center" placeholder="8">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="number" id="electrons2" name="electrons2" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent text-center" placeholder="7">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="number" id="energyLevels2" name="energyLevels2" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent text-center" placeholder="2">
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="py-2 px-3 border border-gray-300">
                                        <input type="text" id="electronsPerLevel2" name="electronsPerLevel2" class="w-full px-2 py-1 border-0 bg-transparent" placeholder="2, 5">
                                    </td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
                
                <div class="mb-6">
                    <div class="mb-2">
                        <label for="conclusion" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold">Conclusion:</label>
                    </div>
                    <textarea id="conclusion" name="conclusion" rows="10" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg resize-y" placeholder="Write your conclusions about the atomic models you constructed. What did you learn about isotopes, energy levels, and electron distribution?"></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="mb-4">
                    <label for="photoUpload" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Upload Photo (optional):</label>
                    <input type="file" id="photoUpload" name="photoUpload" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg">
                </div>
                
                <div class="flex justify-end">
                    <button type="submit" class="bg-blue-600 text-white px-6 py-2 rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-700">Submit Experiment</button>
                </div>
            </form>
        </section>

        <div class="mt-8 text-center">
            <a href="/physical-science" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 text-white px-6 py-2 rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-700">Back to Physical Science</a>
        </div>
    </main>

    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@emailjs/browser@4/dist/email.min.js"></script>
    <script>
        // Initialize EmailJS with error checking
        if (typeof emailjs !== 'undefined') {
            emailjs.init("ct_s19oZHppLy9BlW");
        }

        document.getElementById('experimentForm').addEventListener('submit', function(e) {
            e.preventDefault();
            
            // Get form data
            const formData = {
                studentName: document.getElementById('studentName').value,
                studentEmail: document.getElementById('studentEmail').value,
                elementChosen: document.getElementById('elementChosen').value,
                isotopeAnalysis: document.getElementById('isotopeAnalysis').value,
                conclusion: document.getElementById('conclusion').value
            };
            
            // Send email using EmailJS
            if (typeof emailjs !== 'undefined') {
                emailjs.send('service_ot1jg6s', 'template_y556rhr', {
                    student_name: formData.studentName || formData.studentEmail?.split('@')[0] || 'Student',
                    student_email: formData.studentEmail,
                    experiment_title: 'Experiment 3.1: Constructing 3-D Atomic Models',
                    hypothesis: 'Exploring atomic structure through 3D model construction',
                    missing_elements: `Student Info: ${formData.studentName ? '✓' : '✗'}, Email: ${formData.studentEmail ? '✓' : '✗'}, Element: ${formData.elementChosen ? '✓' : '✗'}, Conclusion: ${formData.conclusion ? '✓' : '✗'}`,
                    conclusion: `EXPERIMENT 3.1: CONSTRUCTING 3-D ATOMIC MODELS - STUDENT RESULTS

Student Information:
• Name: ${formData.studentName || formData.studentEmail?.split('@')[0] || 'Student'}
• Email: ${formData.studentEmail}

Experiment Results:

Element Chosen: ${formData.elementChosen || 'Not specified'}
Isotope Analysis: ${formData.isotopeAnalysis || 'Not provided'}

Conclusion:
${formData.conclusion || 'No conclusion provided'}

---
This is an automated submission from the C00lnerd Experiment System.`,
                    subject: `Physical Science Experiment 3.1 Results - ${formData.studentName || formData.studentEmail?.split('@')[0] || 'Student'}`
                })
                .then(function(response) {
                    console.log('SUCCESS!', response.status, response.text);
                    alert('Your experiment has been submitted successfully!');
                }, function(error) {
                    console.log('FAILED...', error);
                    alert('Failed to submit. Please try again.');
                });
            } else {
                alert('Email service not available. Please try again later.');
            }
        });
    </script>
</Layout>
